{"id":"6602ed4c-eb44-43fa-a4da-c89fb01b0e7a","recipient":"a228afbf-3d9a-4a60-8ce6-ace5f878390a","sender":"a7e55962-d1c0-4e42-9dcc-d29979c73d47","priority":"MESSAGE_PRIORITY_HIGH","timestamp":"2026-09-14T09:57:06.191843700Z","renderDetails":{"messageTitle":"Message from Formatter (research)"},"content":"---**I TESTS FOR LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS TENNIS ELBOW** ---**1 COZENS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Procedure • Patient makes a fist • Elbow flexed to 90 • Forearm pronated • Wrist extended and radially deviated against resistance Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**2 MILLS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Therapist passively flexes the wrist while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**3 MAUDSLEYS TEST MIDDLE FINGER TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis especially involvement of the Extensor Digitorum Communis EDC Procedure • Elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Patient resists extension of the middle finger Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**II TEST FOR MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS GOLFERS ELBOW** ---**4 GOLFERS ELBOW TEST MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS TEST** Purpose To assess medial epicondylitis Golfers Elbow Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm supinated • Therapist passively extends the wrist and fingers while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the medial epicondyle Indicates Medial Epicondylitis Golfers Elbow ---**III NERVE COMPRESSION TEST** ---**5 TINELS SIGN CUBITAL TUNNEL** Purpose To assess ulnar nerve irritation or compression Procedure • Slightly flex the elbow • Tap gently over the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation along the ring and little fingers Indicates Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Ulnar nerve entrapment ---**IV LIGAMENT STABILITY TESTS** ---**6 VALGUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Medial Ulnar Collateral Ligament MCL/UCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a valgus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive medial joint opening Indicates Medial collateral ligament injury ---**7 VARUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Lateral Collateral Ligament LCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a varus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive lateral joint opening Indicates Lateral collateral ligament injury","sourceMetadata":{"tool":{"conversationId":"a7e55962-d1c0-4e42-9dcc-d29979c73d47","stepIndex":4,"toolCall":{"id":"call_8792527","name":"send_message","argumentsJson":"{\"Message\":\"---**I TESTS FOR LATERAL EPICONDYLITIS TENNIS ELBOW** ---**1 COZENS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Procedure • Patient makes a fist • Elbow flexed to 90 • Forearm pronated • Wrist extended and radially deviated against resistance Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**2 MILLS TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Therapist passively flexes the wrist while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**3 MAUDSLEYS TEST MIDDLE FINGER TEST** Purpose To assess lateral epicondylitis especially involvement of the Extensor Digitorum Communis EDC Procedure • Elbow extended • Forearm pronated • Patient resists extension of the middle finger Positive Sign Pain over the lateral epicondyle Indicates Lateral epicondylitis ---**II TEST FOR MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS GOLFERS ELBOW** ---**4 GOLFERS ELBOW TEST MEDIAL EPICONDYLITIS TEST** Purpose To assess medial epicondylitis Golfers Elbow Procedure • Patient sits with the elbow extended • Forearm supinated • Therapist passively extends the wrist and fingers while maintaining elbow extension Positive Sign Pain over the medial epicondyle Indicates Medial Epicondylitis Golfers Elbow ---**III NERVE COMPRESSION TEST** ---**5 TINELS SIGN CUBITAL TUNNEL** Purpose To assess ulnar nerve irritation or compression Procedure • Slightly flex the elbow • Tap gently over the ulnar nerve behind the medial epicondyle Positive Sign Tingling or electric shock sensation along the ring and little fingers Indicates Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Ulnar nerve entrapment ---**IV LIGAMENT STABILITY TESTS** ---**6 VALGUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Medial Ulnar Collateral Ligament MCL/UCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a valgus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive medial joint opening Indicates Medial collateral ligament injury ---**7 VARUS STRESS TEST** Purpose To assess the Lateral Collateral Ligament LCL Procedure • Elbow slightly flexed 20 30 • Stabilize the humerus • Apply a varus force to the forearm Positive Sign Pain or excessive lateral joint opening Indicates Lateral collateral ligament injury\",\"Recipient\":\"a228afbf-3d9a-4a60-8ce6-ace5f878390a\",\"toolAction\":\"Sending formatted text to parent\",\"toolSummary\":\"Send text back\"}","thinkingSignature":""}}}}